<h2>The dispute: two nursing home sites in Chandigarh</h2>
<p>The Chandigarh Administration e-auctioned residential, commercial and nursing home sites on a freehold basis. Two nursing home sites carried a reserve price of ₹6.91 crore each. Bedi Hospital and another bidder competed; a bid was accepted for Site No. 4, Sector 33C. The Administration then had second thoughts: the accepted bid, it concluded, did not reflect market trends. In 2013 it cancelled the bid contract, and later the agreement to sell, refunding the consideration. Bedi Hospital challenged the cancellations in the Punjab and Haryana High Court and succeeded — the writ court quashed the cancellation letters. The successful bidder in the fresh auction, and the Administration, carried the matter to the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The question: when does the bidder\'s right crystallise?</h2>
<p>The legal frame was supplied by the Chandigarh Estate Rules, 2007 and the e-auction terms. The Court\'s analysis proceeded in two steps.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Before conveyance deed</h4><p>Bid accepted; agreement to sell executed under Rule 5(iv). This is <em>not</em> an enforceable contract under Section 2(h) of the Contract Act. The authority may withdraw the site under Rule 5(vi) read with the auction terms, and may revisit the process to maximise price.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>After conveyance deed</h4><p>An enforceable contract exists. The bidder\'s rights vest, and the authority can no longer recall the auction merely to seek a better price. Any interference must find some other lawful basis.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Since the conveyance deed for Site No. 4 had never been executed, the Administration retained the power to act — and the reason it gave, that the price was substantially below market trends, was a relevant and legitimate one.</p>

<h2>Fairness remains the touchstone</h2>
<p>The Court did not hand public authorities a free pass. Its reasoning rests on the twin duties of a public seller: to deal fairly with bidders, and to obtain the best price for public property. On the facts, both were satisfied:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>The cancellation letter recorded the reason for rescission rather than leaving the bidder guessing.</li>
    <li>The full consideration was refunded on the same day as the cancellation.</li>
    <li>The displaced bidder was permitted to participate in the fresh auction, and chose not to.</li>
    <li>The decision pursued a public purpose — price maximisation — not favouritism toward any rival.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Finding no illegality or arbitrariness, the Court allowed the appeals, set aside the High Court\'s judgment, and directed the Administration to conduct a fresh e-auction of the site, to commence and conclude within three months.</p>

<h2>What the ruling means in commercial practice</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>For</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Bidders at public auctions</td><td>Identify the precise document that creates the enforceable contract under the governing rules — allotment letters and agreements to sell may confer far less than they appear to. Until that document exists, budget for the risk of a re-auction.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Public authorities</td><td>A decision to re-auction should be supported by material showing the bid\'s inadequacy, a recorded reason, a prompt refund, and an open door for the displaced bidder to bid again.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Financiers of auction purchases</td><td>Disbursement milestones tied to the conveyance deed, not the bid acceptance, better reflect when the purchaser\'s right actually vests.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Litigators</td><td>A writ against cancellation must engage with the rule structure: if no enforceable contract existed, the challenge must establish arbitrariness or mala fides, not merely the fact of acceptance.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The judgment continues a consistent judicial theme: public property is held in trust, and the citizen\'s legitimate expectation at an auction is of a fair process, not of a windfall locked in before the state\'s pen touches the conveyance deed.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The governing rules differ from state to state and transaction to transaction.</p></div>
